Yes. The interesting move is to make the advertising mechanism itself the content. Wild Hawg doesn’t need to explain the gummy yet; it can behave like a self-aware creature loose inside TikTok.

I’d distinguish this from a literal Kafka trap—where disagreement is treated as proof. What you want is more like a self-referential engagement loop: seeing, scrolling, commenting, getting targeted, or recognizing the joke makes the viewer part of the bit.

Some strong families:

* Paid-to-find-you
    * “If you can see this, I paid to meet you.”
    * “Congratulations. You were expensive.”
    * “TikTok said you’d like this. Defend yourself.”
    * “We gave TikTok money and apparently it bought us you.”
    * “This ad has traveled farther than our product has.”
    * “Technically, you’re part of our marketing budget now.”
    * “I don’t know who you are. The algorithm does.”
    * “If this says Sponsored, we specifically went looking for trouble.”
    * “We paid money to interrupt whatever you were doing. Hi.”
    * “Our first customer acquisition cost was apparently your attention.”
* The scroll trap
    * “You may scroll. That is one of the predicted outcomes.”
    * “Scroll if you hate being told to scroll.”
    * “Don’t engage with this post.”
    * “Whatever you do, do NOT teach the algorithm that people like this.”
    * “Ignore this. We’re testing something.”
    * “This post becomes stronger every time somebody tries to kill it.”
    * “If everyone scrolls, this disappears. You know what to do.”
    * “This is not reverse psychology. Unless that worked better.”
* Algorithm as a character
    * “The algorithm has selected another candidate.”
    * “TikTok thinks you’re Wild Hawg material. We have no idea why.”
    * “The machine brought you here. We just work here.”
    * “Our marketing department is currently one algorithm and a bad idea.”
    * “We asked TikTok to find people with questionable judgment.”
    * “Algorithm: ‘Trust me, show it to this guy.’”
    * “Wild Hawg has entered your recommendation system.”
    * “You were not supposed to find us this early.”
    * “Apparently our target demographic is people who stop to read nonsense.”
* Brand-before-product
    * “No product reveal. You haven’t earned the lore yet.”
    * “Imagine following a brand before it has anything to sell you.”
    * “We’re building the cult following before remembering the cult product.”
    * “Day 6 of marketing something you cannot currently buy.”
    * “You can’t buy Wild Hawg yet. Frankly, neither can we.”
    * “We have a logo, caffeine and unreasonable confidence.”
    * “Most companies make the product and then find the audience. Boring.”
    * “Building demand before supply because apparently economics needed a side quest.”
    * “This is either excellent marketing or evidence we need adult supervision.”
* Make the viewer an early character
    * “Screenshot this so you can lie later and say you were here first.”
    * “You’re seeing Wild Hawg before it was cool. Act accordingly.”
    * “The first 1,000 people get nothing except unbearable superiority.”
    * “Congratulations, you have discovered pre-history.”
    * “There will eventually be people pretending they knew us this early.”
    * “When this becomes a thing, become insufferable about this screenshot.”
    * “Founding members receive zero equity and unlimited smugness.”
    * “If Wild Hawg dies, nobody ever has to know you followed us.”

That last category is powerful because the reward is identity rather than product. You’re letting somebody buy socially into the story with essentially zero friction.

Then there are ones where the comments become part of the experiment:

“Do not comment ‘hogg.’ We’re trying to determine whether humans can follow extremely simple instructions.”

Or:

“Every comment makes it slightly harder for us to pretend this marketing strategy was an accident.”

And:

“We need 100 people to comment something completely unrelated so the next person has absolutely no idea what is happening.”

That creates what I’d call manufactured archaeological layers. Someone arrives later, sees 700 comments saying “refrigerator,” “Taxachusetts,” or 🐗, and immediately thinks: What the hell happened here? Curiosity becomes the acquisition funnel.

Another particularly good pattern for Wild Hawg is weaponized disclosure:

“ADVERTISEMENT.
There. Now that we’ve established neither of us respects the illusion, what’s up?”

Or:

“Companies spend millions trying to make advertisements not feel like advertisements.
This is an advertisement.”

Or:

“We would like to manipulate you into remembering the words WILD HAWG.
Unfortunately we have now explained the trick.”

That paradoxically builds trust because you’re revealing the machinery while operating the machinery. The fourth wall becomes the brand voice.

You can get even stranger with recursive ones:

“This post is targeting people who think they’re too smart to be influenced by advertising.”

That one is nearly an actual Kafka trap, so I’d keep the punchline on the brand, not make the viewer feel insulted:

“This post is targeting people who think they’re too smart for advertising. Unfortunately, that’s also whoever wrote this.”

Better.

One Wild Hawg mechanic I’d actually build

Create an ongoing series called “THE EXPERIMENT.”

Every post is numbered, but don’t initially explain what is being tested:

EXPERIMENT 0047
“If you can see this, TikTok believes you have something in common with the last 46 groups. We aren’t telling you what.”

Then:

EXPERIMENT 0048
“We changed one variable.”

Then:

EXPERIMENT 0051
“Something went wrong with 0049. Do not look for it.”

There doesn’t even have to be a 0049.

Now you’ve introduced missing information as content. People investigate the profile, compare posts, comment theories, send them to friends, and accidentally learn the brand while trying to solve a nonexistent mystery.

You can layer Wild Hawg lore into it slowly:

EXPERIMENT 0066
“Introducing caffeine appears to have made the subjects considerably more cooperative.”

Then eventually the gummy appears.

The deeper principle is:

Don’t advertise the object yet. Advertise participation in the emergence of the object.

That gives Wild Hawg something most startup consumer brands don’t have: history before launch. People aren’t merely watching an energy gummy company advertise. They’re watching a weird little thing become real.

And that is exactly where your “marketing web” idea gets interesting: every ad can be a different entrance into the same mythology—paid targeting, algorithm jokes, unexplained symbols, experiments, fake arguments with the brand account, audience-created lore, product prototypes, etc.—and you can measure which doorway causes people to walk deeper rather than merely which post gets views.